\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
The PDC Web Client
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Alexander Bonomo
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This document describes and outlines the web client for the Personal Data
 Cloud.
 A user's PDC
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "PDC"
description "Personal Data Cloud"

\end_inset

 is composed of one or more secure repositories of data, also known as Personal
 Data Vaults (PDVs).
 The PDC runs on top of NDN
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "NDN"
description "Named Data Networking"

\end_inset

.
 This document also provides example use cases for using the web client.
 It describes in detail both how the web client serves web pages to the
 user on the front end, as well as how it communicates with the PDC via
 NDN on the back end.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset nomencl_print
LatexCommand printnomenclature
set_width "none"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PDC Web Client allows a user to connect to a particular PDV instance.
 Once authenticated and connected to the PDV instance, a user can use the
 web client to download or upload data from the PDV.
 Perhaps more importantly, the user can also use the web client to manage
 their data and configure security settings for the data.
 This includes but is not limited to setting a publisher and adding receivers
 to data streams, as well as adding and removing apps and data streams.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Front End
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the front end, the web client serves HTTP web pages to the user.
 It does this using the Jetty web server API for Java.
 Each web page displayed to a user is done so by the use of servlets.
 Servlets receive an HTTP request, process any necessary data, then display
 HTML to the user.
 For example, the home page servlet receives the user's login credentials,
 then checks the credentials with the PDC.
 If the user is authenticated to log in, the home page will be displayed,
 otherwise the login has failed, and the user will be redirected to try
 to log in again.
 Each servlet is registered to the web server for a particular URL namespace.
 Thus each HTML web page accessed in the web client is served to the user
 by the appropriate servlet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Back End
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the back end, the web client communicates with the PDC via NDN.
 There is a class for each type of data on the PDC.
 For example, the PDCApps class lists, adds, and removes the applications
 for the user on this PDV instance.
 These commands are executed by sending interests to the appropriate namespace
 in the PDV instance.
 The PDV instance then responds with an appropriate data packet.
 There are also a couple classes that handle interests and provide data
 to the PDC, namely the user's login credentials.
 Thus this portion of the software is essentially used to communicate with
 the PDV instance.
 These classes are the level of indirection used by the front end for communicat
ion with the PDC.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Other Features of the Software
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A couple other features of the software include a logging service, a customized
 session manager, and a library of tools and utilities to facilitate development.
 The logging service runs on a separate thread, so as not to interrupt normal
 execution of the web client.
 It handles requests to write to the log.
 The session manager manages all the sessions currently logged in on a PDV
 instance.
 Each session contains information about the user's session.
 For example, the URI of the PDV instance which they are connected to is
 stored in the Session instance.
 The Session instance also contains the symmetric key and any information
 which the user has selected (e.g.
 if they have selected an application, datastream, etc.).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The webClient.lib package contains various tools and utilities to aid the
 current and any future development of the web client.
 The Algorithms class houses static algorithms for others to use, such as
 an MD5 hash.
 The Security class is similar to the Algorithms class in that it also houses
 static functions for others to use; however, these functions are for security
 purposes (e.g.
 generating a public/private key pair).
 The lib package also contains classes to encapsulate data that needs to
 be transmitted via NDN.
 This includes basic implementations of iBSONExportable and CCNFilterListener,
 which can be extended to specific uses.
 A few examples of these extentions are classes used for the logging in
 steps of the protocol (Credentials and AuthenticationResponse), as well
 as a CollectionTransport class which can be used to transmit a collection
 of objects via NDN.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Use Cases
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following sections describe how a user would use the web client to interact
 with the PDC to download data and to modify their datastream configuration
 settings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Downloading Data
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Downloading-Data-Use-Case"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user first navigates to the URL of the web client.
 A page is displayed where they provide the location (an NDN URI) of a PDV
 instance to connect to and a hexadecimal representation of the PDV's public
 key.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user then provides his username and password as credentials of his/her
 identity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user then selects the download link on the home page.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A list of applications will then be presented upon which the user will select
 the desired app to pull data from.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A list of datastreams will then be presented.
 The user will select which data stream they want to download from.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A list of data records for the stream will then be presented.
 The user can then select the records which they want to download.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Configuring Settings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user first navigates to the URL of the web client.
 A page is displayed where they provide the location (an NDN URI) of a PDV
 instance to connect to and a hexadecimal representation of the PDV's public
 key.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user then provides his username and password as credentials of his/her
 identity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The user then selects the configure link on the home page.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A list of applications will then be presented upon which the user will select
 the desired app.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A list of datastreams will then be presented.
 The user will select which data stream they want to configure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A page is displayed that allows a user to set the publisher for the data
 stream or add a receiver to the data stream.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Protocols and Commands
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Workflow
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a user first connects to the Web Client, they must provide the URI
 of a PDV instance to connect to as well as that PDV instance's public key.
 The user then provides their logon credentials (i.e.
 username and password combination).
 The public key is only used during the logon process to encrypt the user's
 credentials and to securely establish a symmetric key.
 This symmetric key is then used to encrypt all data between the PDV and
 the WC.
 If authenticated, a user is then directed to a home page from which they
 can manage the given PDV instance.
\end_layout

